# Copy this file to frontend/.env and set the value for your own machine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env)
#
# .env is git-ignored on purpose. This setting is the address *your browser*
# reaches *your backend* on, so there is no one value that is correct for
# everybody -- committing one machine's address points every other developer's
# frontend at that machine.
#
# Where the API lives. `python app.py` binds 0.0.0.0:5000, so the port is
# always 5000; only the host differs. Note this is the backend's port -- the
# Vite dev server this page is served from is 5173, and the two are separate.
#
#   Working on your own machine only:
#     VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5000/api
#
#   Opening the app from another machine on the same network -- a phone, or a
#   staff member following a password-reset link -- use this machine's LAN
#   address instead. 127.0.0.1 would mean *their* machine, where nothing is
#   listening, and the connection is refused before any route is considered.
#   `ipconfig` (Windows) or `ip addr` (Linux/macOS) shows the address:
#     VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.50:5000/api
#
# If you use a LAN address, match it in backend/config/dev.ini -- cors_origins
# and frontend_base_url -- or the browser is refused by CORS and the reset
# links the portal emails point somewhere unreachable.
#
# Restart the dev server after changing this. Vite reads env files only at
# startup, so an edit here does nothing until `npm run dev` is run again.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5000/api
